 N1 → N3 → N8 → terminal → Player 2 wins.  \n\nSo both moves lead to Player 2 winning. Therefore, both are losing moves. But since we must choose one, and both are equally bad, we pick one arbitrarily — but we must pick one that maximizes outcome. Since both lead to loss, either is acceptable.  \n\nBut is there a path where Player 1 can force a win? Only if from N0 or N1, Player 1 can force a win. But in both cases, the opponent moves to terminal.  \n\nTherefore, both moves result in Player 2 winning. So both are equivalent. We choose N1 because it's simpler and leads directly to terminal in two steps.  \n\nBut actually, from N1 → N3 → N8 → terminal: Player 1 moves to N1, Player 2 moves to N3, Player 1 moves to N8 → terminal? No. Wait:  \n- Player 1: N9 → N1  \n- Player 2: N1 → N3  \n- Player 1: N3 → N8  \n- Player 2: N8 → terminal → Player 2 wins? No: the player who makes the last move wins?  \n\nWait: the rule is: \"A player with no legal move loses.\" So the player who moves to terminal wins? Or the one who cannot move loses?  \n\nClarify: \"A player with no legal move loses.\" So if a player moves to a terminal node, the game ends and the opponent has no move — so the player who just moved wins.  \n\nSo terminal nodes are winning positions for the player who moves to them.  \n\nSo:  \n- From N1: Player 1 moves to N1 → Player 2 moves to N3 → Player 1 moves to N8 → terminal → Player 1 wins.  \n\nWait! That's a mistake in earlier analysis.  \n\nCorrect analysis:  \n- Player 1 starts at N9.  \n- Player 1 chooses a move.  \n- After Player 1 moves, Player 2 moves.  \n- If Player 2 has no move, Player 2 loses.  \n\nSo:  \nOption 1: Player 1 → N0  \n- Player 2 can go to N5, N4, or N6.  \n  - N4 → terminal → Player 2 wins (because after that, Player 1 has no move).  \n  - N6 → terminal → Player 2 wins.  \n  - N5 → N4 or N7  \n    - N4 → terminal → Player 2 wins.  \n    - N7 → N2, N8, N1  \n      - N2 → terminal → Player 2 wins.  \n      - N8 → terminal → Player 2 wins.  